# ThreadSanitizer suppressions for the uninstrumented host audio stack (#1219).
#
# Loaded by the tests-tsan-local and tests-tsan-host ctest presets, which
# `python yse.py test --sanitizer tsan [--host-audio]` runs. CI's tests-tsan
# and tests-tsan-full presets don't load it. Their runners have no sound card,
# so these libraries never open a stream there.
#
# libasound, libpipewire and libspa are not built with -fsanitize=thread, so
# TSan cannot see the synchronisation inside them. On a desktop whose PortAudio
# reaches a live PipeWire server, it then reports races and lock problems
# between threads that those libraries order themselves.
#
# Rules:
#   - Every pattern names a library function or module, never a YSE frame.
#   - No called_from_lib: for libpipewire or libspa. That would make TSan
#     ignore their malloc/free too, which also hides YSE heap bugs that land
#     in blocks those libraries allocate or free. #1222 showed up exactly that
#     way: an over-read in channelManager.cpp:279 reported against a free
#     in pw_properties_clear. Such reports must stay visible.
#   - Don't add a suppression for a report whose two stacks both start in YSE
#     code. #1223 is one such report, from the live-audio run, and it is a
#     YSE bug.

# paCallback (PortAudio's ALSA callback thread) writes the mmap'd period
# buffer; PipeWire's data-loop thread copies it out under a libspa mutex.
race:snd_pcm_area_copy

# Lock-order inversions between libasound's and libspa's own mutexes, taken on
# PortAudio's and PipeWire's threads under Pa_OpenStream / Pa_Initialize.
deadlock:libspa-support.so
deadlock:libasound.so

# Double lock and destroy-of-a-locked-mutex inside libspa-support, under
# Pa_Initialize / Pa_OpenStream / refreshDevices(). The device-refresh suite
# reaches these even with the audio server hidden.
mutex:libspa-support.so
